  "query_preview": "..Look for a mainly dry weather pattern well into next\nweek.\n\nThe rest of this afternoon and overnight...A weak and fast moving\nshortwave trough will brush the Four Corners area. With the\npassage of this trough look for a few clouds from the Mogollon Rim\nnorthward. A stray light shower or two will be possible across \nthe area from Black Mesa to the Chuska Mountains through early \nthis evening. In addition, look for gusty southwest winds at 10-20\nmph gusting to 25-35 mph along and south of a Flagstaff-Window \nRock line, diminishing rapidly after sunset.\n\nOn Thursday...A weak and dry flow develops aloft leading to light\nand variable surface winds at 5-10 mph under mainly clear skies. \nLittle change in high/low temperatures.\n\nFrom Friday into Sunday...An additional weak trough drops\nsouthward across Arizona on Friday before heading further\nsouth on Saturday and Sunday. As the trough passes through on \nFriday, there will be just enough lingering moisture and cool air\naloft for the development of instability showers over a few\nhigher terrain areas with daytime heating. Consequently, a slight\nchance of showers is expected over the Mogollon Rim, White \nMountains and Chuska Mountains Friday afternoon into the early \nevening. Precipitation amounts, if any, will be minimal. Behind \nthe trough on Saturday and Sunday high pressure will build over \nthe Great Basin. Pressure gradients for north to northeast flow \nwill then develop with locally gusty winds developing. Most areas \nwill see north to northeast winds at 5-15 mph but for locations \nwhere northeast winds get funneled along and south to the Mogollon\nRim, local gusts of 20-30 mph will be possible.\n\nFrom Monday onward...It now looks like fair and mainly dry \nweather will hold through at least next Wednesday or possibly even\nlonger.",
